From: Barrett Sonntag <bsonntag@salesforce.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 19:34:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] docs(guardrail): clarify optional semantic provider
 policy

---
 docs/adr/0052-sf-guardrail-is-rule-behavior-only.md | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/adr/0052-sf-guardrail-is-rule-behavior-only.md b/docs/adr/0052-sf-guardrail-is-rule-behavior-only.md
index 3b2dd397220b3f627db3bb9ccb5c3ff2e0702fb4..299d186676f25481ac44f22265df9333c2aaa66d 100644
--- a/docs/adr/0052-sf-guardrail-is-rule-behavior-only.md
+++ b/docs/adr/0052-sf-guardrail-is-rule-behavior-only.md
@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@ supersedes: ["0044", "0045"]
 
 # SF Guardrail is rule-behavior only
 
+> Updated 2026-09-21 by [ADR 0118](./0118-sf-guardrail-supports-an-optional-local-jev-risk-provider.md): exact user policy constraints remain authoritative, with per-rule Rule Behavior as the policy control. ADR 0118 now describes optional local semantic-provider decisions and operator-controlled provider modes; those decisions cannot override exact user policy.
+
 SF Guardrail no longer has bulk posture modes, hard-block themes, broad feature master switches, or an internal enabled flag. When the extension is enabled, mediation is controlled by per-rule Rule Behavior only: `off`, `confirm`, or `block`.
 
 The complete disable path is the SF Pi package lifecycle control: disable the `sf-guardrail` extension from the Manager Surface or with `/sf-pi disable sf-guardrail`. This avoids the ambiguous state where the extension is loaded but a hidden internal switch makes it inert.

